{"kind":"assert","text":"Amendment to design request #676 (jsonata-ddl-core), from a two-repo survey of what gitseq's more sophisticated folds would demand. Key empirical finding: gitseq's PRODUCTION fold (internal/workroom, workroom-fold@15, ~3500 lines) uses NO JSONata at all — it is hand-written Go doing multi-event-type lifecycle state machines, cross-record correlation, whole-log accumulated indices, DAG closure for staleness/lineage, position-versioned vocabulary, and fingerprint-based independent-authority verification on merge receipts. gitseq's OWN native-extension design (notes/2026-08-27-jsonata-ddl-stable-extensions.md) explicitly forbids native helpers from producing effects or reading the log (its Rule 5), so those log-spanning parts can NEVER become native functions. The design note for #676 should therefore adopt a THREE-TIER framing of fold sophistication and place the library boundary between the tiers:\n\nTIER 1 — declarative richness the pure-JSONata deterministic model can already hold: multiple private event types, multiple reads per fold including bounded READ ... MANY MAX n ORDER BY, and multi-table projection graphs (gitseq's chess app needs all three). Tailapp already flags its single-otel_event restriction as \"an intentional version-one simplification, not a requirement of the two-stage architecture\" (architecture note deferred-scope). Treat Tier 1 as IN-SCOPE shared-library work that benefits both consumers and does not touch determinism.\n\nTIER 2 — bounded native scalar helpers (CREATE FUNCTION / CREATE CONTEXT): chess adjudication, constant-time SHA-256, exact-event identity context. This is the \"custom code composition\" risk. It is a whole subsystem, not a hook: capability registry keyed by meaning, per-call metering in a deterministic work unit (not elapsed time), a no-ambient-authority sandbox (no SQL/log/clock/randomness/files/state), canonical result validation before JSONata observes the value, and folding every helper's semantic identity into the fold-version digest (even a bug-fix that changes a result forces a new identity). DESIGN MOVE: the library owns the MECHANISM (contract, metering, sandbox rules, identity composition); each host owns the POLICY (which providers, if any, are registered). Tailapp registers ZERO providers and keeps its closed function allowlist; gitseq registers its set. RESERVE this seam as a versioned contract in the library spec and in the composite identity, but DEFER its implementation until a real second consumer commits — otherwise tailapp (the only near-term consumer) pays a large complexity tax for a feature it deliberately deferred (architecture note: \"native fold helpers or other extension capabilities\").\n\nTIER 3 — irreducibly hand-written Go: commitment lifecycle, staleness closure, merge-receipt authority. gitseq's own Rule 5 makes these permanently ineligible for native functions; they live ABOVE any fold-evaluation library. Record Tier 3 as permanently host-side.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE COMPOSITE RUNTIME IDENTITY (the anti-divergence core of #676): the identity needs a FOURTH dimension beyond library-component x host-canonicalization x host-SQL-surface, namely host-registered-capability-set, because a host that registers native helpers has a materially different replay contract. gitseq's note already folds native-helper identity into the application package digest, so this is compatible with their model.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE OPEN QUESTION \"will gitseq promote its spike to production\": because Tier 3 can never move into the library, adopting jsonata-ddl-core would NEVER mean replacing workroom-fold@15 — that logic is structurally ineligible. At most gitseq uses the library for NEW natively-declarative applications (chess-like). So the honest expected value of the library for gitseq is \"a substrate for future simple apps\", not \"the engine under the production fold\" — which argues for keeping the library Tier-1-plus-reserved-seam and resisting absorption of Tier 2/3, conveniently also what keeps tailapp lean. The note should still pose the question to the operator, but with this framing attached. None of this changes the request's conditions; it sharpens what the note must decide."}